        "text": "\n\n\nThis happens"
      }, {
        "text": " because of how microcontrollers handle \"the end\" of a program compared to standard computers. \n\nIn your code, there"
      }, {
        "text": " is no `while(1)` loop at the end. Because the CPU runs at 16 Megahertz, it"
      }, {
        "text": " processes `ao()`, `keep_up()`, and `lcd(\"Relese\")` in a fraction of a millisecond,"
      }, {
        "text": " and then it **reaches the end of the `void main()` function and exits.**\n\nWhen an Arduino/AVR microcontroller"
      }, {
        "text": " exits `main()`, it executes an internal kill command (`exit(0)`) which instantly **turns off all hardware interrupts**"
      }, {
        "text": " to halt the CPU. Because `Serial.print` uses interrupts to send text character-by-character in the background, the CPU halted"
      }, {
        "text": " right as it sent the `"
      }, {
        "text": ""
      }, {
        "text": ""
      }, {
        "text": ""
      }, {
        "text": ""
      }, {
        "text": ""
      }, {
        "text": ""
      }, {
        "text": ""
      }, {
        "text": ""
      }, {
