        "text": "\n\n\nThis is a classic problem with AVR/Arduino! \n\nTo"
      }, {
        "text": " save Flash Memory space, the standard Arduino core **disables floating-point support (`%f`) in the `sprintf` and"
      }, {
        "text": " `vsnprintf` functions by default.** Whenever you pass a float to `%f` on an Arduino, it just"
      }, {
        "text": " prints a `?`. In the legacy Krumon environment, they manually enabled the `-lprintf_flt` linker flag to"
      }, {
        "text": " force it to work, but standard cloud compilers don\u0027t have that enabled.\n\nTo fix this seamlessly, I have rewritten the invisible"
      }, {
        "text": " Krumon `lcd()` library function. Instead of relying on the stripped-down Arduino `printf` engine, I wrote a custom format"
      }, {
        "text": " parser that manually catches `%f`, `%d`, `%02d`, etc., and natively unpacks them using Arduino\u0027s `dt"
      }, {
        "text": "ostrf()` (Double to String Format). \n\nThis completely bypasses the hardware limitation, so your `lcd(\" A \u003d %d"
      }, {
        "text": " , B \u003d %.2f\", a, b);` will now output exactly as expected.\n\nHere is the fully updated"
      }, {
