      "parts": [{
        "text": "\n\n\nThis is a fantastic addition. Real-world IR sensors are basically infrared LEDs bouncing light off a surface. If"
      }, {
        "text": " you point them straight down, they read lines on a map (Line Trackers). If you point them forward, they bounce"
      }, {
        "text": " off physical objects (Obstacle Detectors).\n\nI have added a **\"Mode\"** dropdown specifically for the IR Sensor. \n\nHere"
      }, {
        "text": " is what this update brings:\n1.  **Dual Modes:** You can now toggle any IR sensor between `Map (Line)"
      }, {
        "text": "` and `Object`. \n2.  **Object Detection Physics:** When set to `Object`, the sensor ignores the canvas"
      }, {
        "text": " floor map and instead fires a short 40-pixel raycast outward. If it hits any physical `Matter.js` prop"
      }, {
        "text": "/wall, it triggers. \n3.  **Visual Indicators:** If an IR sensor is set to Object mode, a short transparent"
      }, {
        "text": " beam will be drawn extending out of it so you can see exactly where its 40px blindspot/detection range is.\n4."
      }, {
        "text": "  **Analog Support:** Just like the real world, the Object mode is analog-capable. If you connect it to an Analog"
      }, {
        "text": " pin (like `A0`), it will return a proportional voltage based on how close the object is (0 to 4"
      }, {
        "text": "0px). If you put it on a digital pin, it simply flips `HIGH` when something is in range.\n\nHere is the fully updated"
      }, {
        "text": " `App.jsx`.\n\n--- START OF FILE App.jsx ---\n\n```javascript\nimport React, { useState, useRef"
      }, {
        "text": ", useEffect } from \u0027react\u0027;\nimport Editor from \u0027@monaco-editor/react\u0027;\nimport { Play,"
      }, {
